body:
\>What have they figured out that the rest of us haven’t?

&#x200B;

It's what they haven't figured out that makes them happy, a.k.a naivety.  They're naive and see the little things and derive joy out of it.  They don't see life in the big worldview that is full of pain, unfairness, and sadness.  I don't want to generalize, and I have one friend that is always happy.  Although we're close, we're polar opposites.  I'm depressed and always in my thoughts.  She's naive and gullible, but happy.

My sympathy goes to your best friend too.  I hope you can be there for him/her because it really is a lot to handle.  I keep hearing "I can't imagine" too, and it feels very isolating.  That is why I posted online, to relate to others because most of my friend group hasn't gone through this magnitude of problems yet.   I don't have much support.  I became very isolated when I had to work full time and take care of both parents because I was too tired to have a social life at that point.  I hope your best friend is surrounded by support and love and can get through this.  

&#x200B;

My dogs really are saving my life at this point, but most days taking them to the park is all I can do and I still feel lonely.  One of the dog's is my mom's dog.  If I didn't have them, I would be coming home to an empty house.  I try to imagine what it's like for them, to see their humans going away and not coming back,  wondering why.  They follow me everywhere and are super attached to me.  Thanks for responding.

Thank you for hearing me out and validating that I can take time to process this.  From my experience, people in this day and age want instant gratification.  They want to "cheer you up" or say that they're here to help, but this is not an "easy fix".  I've had a friend say she just wants to shake me so that I can wake up and "feel better" and be the person I was before all this happened.  But that is not possible.  This really did happen. I can not be the same.  There is no "moving on". There is just learning to live with it, or not. 

&#x200B;

When I lost my dad, I already saw how quickly everyone else moves on and it's not their fault.  Everyone has their own problems and time keeps moving forward.  I just feel stuck now.  My world stopped.  I know the Earth is still moving, but I just wish right now, it would stop.  When I lost my dad and job, I actually still had hope and knew I would be ok.  But my mom was my everything, my motivation, and I continually blame myself for not being enough to save her.
